# THE LAYER DIMENSION AS THE CHECKER REACHES IT. One row per filed layer: how many rules
# examine it, how many found a violation, and whether anything reaches it at all.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ IT IS `rank/incidence_cover.sqlc` TRANSPOSED, AND THE TRANSPOSE IS THE PART THAT NAMES
#    THINGS. That relation counts, per RELATION, how much of the layer dimension it touches:
#    `n reaches 49 of 51`. It is an aggregate per relation, so it can say how many and never
#    WHICH, and a layer nothing reaches leaves no row anywhere to be counted. This is the same
#    measurement read down the other axis, where the subject is the layer and the shortfall has
#    a name. ⭐ Third member of the cover family, after `incidence_cover` and `guard_cover`.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND IT COULD NOT HAVE BEEN WRITTEN BEFORE `checks/grain.sqlc` GREW ITS SECOND ARM. Three
#    rules put a claim ADDRESS in the contract's `layer` column, so 324 of the 373 distinct
#    `(filing, layer)` pairs the checker returned named no layer. This relation joined on that
#    key would have kept 49 and dropped 324 and printed a believable table. ⚠️ The first
#    measurement taken that way said TWO layers were unreachable; with the key repaired it is
#    ZERO, so the finding was an artifact of the mis-declared subject rather than a fact about
#    the model. A cover measured through a key that does not resolve measures the key.
#
# ⭐⭐ SO THE USEFUL READING IS DEPTH AND NOT A BOOLEAN. Every filed layer is reached by at least
#    one rule, which makes `reaches = 0` a guard that can fire rather than a finding today. What
#    varies is how MUCH: a layer one rule can speak about is a layer one repair silences, and
#    that is visible here and nowhere else.
#
# ⚠️ `examined` COUNTS RULES AND NOT ROWS, on purpose. A claim-grained rule returns many rows for
#   one layer, and counting those would grade a layer by how many claims it happens to file
#   rather than by how many different questions this repository can put to it.
#
# ⛔ A RULE THAT EXAMINED NOTHING IS NOT A RULE THAT REACHES THIS LAYER. `checks/all.sqlc` joins
#   its roster first so a rule with an empty population still returns a row, with `violates`
#   NULL and no filing. Those rows carry no layer, so they cannot arrive here, which is the
#   ⛔ VACUOUS verdict staying where it belongs: a fact about the rule, not about the layer.
-- checks/all.sqlc counted per layer, against layers/every_layer.sqlc as the whole dimension.
SELECT l.filing,
       l.layer,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule)                                   AS examined_by,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) FILTER (WHERE c.violates)         AS violated,
       count(DISTINCT c.rule) = 0                               AS nothing_reaches_it
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) l
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = l.filing AND c.layer = l.layer
GROUP BY l.filing, l.layer
